{
 "description": "A 300 mm square frame, 340 mm tall, of 20 mm rails held together by screws alone, the way a Creality Ender 5 frame is. Every joint is an M5 socket head screw that goes in through a slot of one rail, across its core, and into the tapped end of the next. The rails are cut for that as techsquid-extrusions builds them. The cross rails have both ends tapped and two screw holes under each post, from below on the base and from above on the top. The side rails have one screw hole at each end, from the outside. The four posts are V-slot 2040 rails with both ends tapped, and stand in the corners on the ends of the cross rails. A screw hole is a 9.5 mm access hole through the lips and a 5.5 mm hole through the rest, so the head sits on the slot floor. A rail with taps or holes is a part of its own: its name says what is cut into it, and every placement of it has the same cuts. Four techsquid-screws runs then place the 24 screws, Screw only, on points on the slot floors. Sixteen M5 x 25 go 11 mm into the ends of the posts. Eight M5 x 20 go 6 mm into the ends of the cross rails: a post screw crosses each of those ends 10 mm in, and a longer screw would meet it. Every thread is cut, in the rail ends and on the screws. A screw still overlaps the tapped bore it is in by a few cubic millimetres, because the two threads are not cut in step; nothing else touches. Two runs of make-t-nut put four post-assembly nuts in the top slots, for a plate, and two pre-assembly nuts in a V-slot of two posts. Everything stays where the points were when the plugin ran. Each step is one command; `caption` is the text a recording shows for it. A plugin step is a `plugin.run` whose `path` is relative to the repository and whose `approval` is the placeholder `@approval`: tests/examples_test.py fills both in, because the approval changes with every build of the plugin. `expect` is what that test measures: the box and volume of every body, as the build measured them, and that the only bodies that overlap are the 24 screws and the tapped bores they are in.",
